          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            What is called
            %em resurgence theory
            is a mathematical formalization of what in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is known as reading off
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effects"} non-perturbative effects
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"}> S-matrix
            ) in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+series"} asymptotic series
            of a function involving
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+function"} non-analytic
            contributions such as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %msup
                          %mi g
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^{-1/g^2}
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pQFT"} perturbation theory
            produces expansions in powers of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resummation"} resummation
            using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+group"} renormalization group
            one can also capture terms involving
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logarithm"} logarithmic
            contributions of the form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi log
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \log (g/g_0)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons"} instantons
            contribute terms of order
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^{-c/g}
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %msup
                          %mi g
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^{-c/g^2}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taylor+expansion"} Taylor expansion
            is identically zero at
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g = 0
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} nonperturbative
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transseries"} transseries
            is an expansion of a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+series"} power series
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector"} vector
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            whose components are fixed functions of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x=g
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x=g^2
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_1=x
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_2=e^{-c/x}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mn 3
                    %mo =
                    %mi log
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_3=\log(x/x_0)
          %p
            Resurgent functions are functions arising in the analysis of singular points of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/germs"} germs
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+functions"} analytic functions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transseries"} transseries
            that arise in methods for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resummation"} resummation
            that generalize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+summation"} Borel summation
            by looking at the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/obstructions"} obstructions
            for Borel summability such as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalons"} renormalons
          %p
            It turns out that using resurgent transseries, one can obtain under certain conditions information from the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} nonperturbative sector
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            alone. The basic idea is to construct from the perturbative series the terms appearing in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+group"} renormalization group
            equation (RGE) by Callan and Symanzik, to substitute into it the leading terms of an appropriate transseries, and to determine the coefficients so that they match the RGE to the order specified.
          %p
            To check that the method really works one can apply it to simple examples form quantum mechanics where other methods can be used to compute nonperturbative information. For example, for the double well potential, this was done by
            %a{:href => "#JentschuraZinnJustin04"} Jentschura &amp; Zinn-Justin 04
            , and for some other cases by (
            %a{:href => "#DunneUnsal14"}> Dunne-Unsal 14
            ). For applications to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theories"} gauge theories
            and to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            = succeed ");" do
              %a{:href => "#Marino12"} Marino 12
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Dorigoni14"} Dorigoni 14
          %p Thus it seems possible (and sometimes is claimed) that the perturbative series already contains all nonperturbative information. The future will tell.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resummation"} resummation
            %li
